  "worked_solution": "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{\u23df}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{\u23df}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{\u23df}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{\u23df}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{\u23df}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{\u23df}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
